      <div class="faq-list">
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>Which Caterham suspension kit should I choose?</summary>
          <p>For the overwhelming majority of Caterham Sevens — fast road, track days, road-and-track use and Nürburgring trips — a correctly specified Nitron NTR R1 is the right answer. Choose the Nitron NTR R3 if you are actively developing the car and adjusting between events with intent. Choose a Penske 7500 COSA or CODA for a committed programme that values Penske build quality and rebuildability, and the Penske 8700 3-Way for a serious, data-led race programme.</p>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>Is a single-adjustable damper a compromise?</summary>
          <p>No. On a Caterham Seven a single adjuster controls compression and rebound together, fine-tuning the car around a base that has already been calibrated for the application. A correctly specified single-adjustable kit will comprehensively out-drive a more adjustable kit that has been set up generically. The hardware sets the ceiling; the specification decides whether you ever reach it.</p>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>How much does a Caterham coilover kit cost?</summary>
          <p>Guide prices, excluding VAT and shipping: Nitron NTR R1 40mm from £1,579; Nitron NTR R3 40mm from £3,206; Penske 7500 COSA from £3,392 (clicker adjuster from £3,636); Penske 7500 CODA from £3,758 (clicker adjuster from £4,002); Penske 8700 3-Way from £7,156. Prices correct as at July 2026.</p>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>Will new coilovers make my Caterham harsh?</summary>
          <p>Not if the spring rates are right. Any correctly specified performance kit will transform the car's ride, control and precision without being harsh. Harshness usually comes from spring rates chosen without reference to the car, its tyres and its use — or from a linear piston that spikes in force over sharp bumps and kerbs. A digressive front piston blends that force off at the high-speed end while keeping strong low-speed control.</p>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>Do these kits fit an S3, SV or CSR Caterham?</summary>
          <p>Yes. Every kit is specified for the Caterham Seven across the S3, SV and CSR chassis and is supplied with mounting spacers. The specification work behind the kit is what adapts it to your particular car, tyres and use.</p>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>Should I upgrade to Hyperco springs?</summary>
          <p>Hyperco springs are rated more accurately and tested at more positions than other coilover springs, and carry a lifetime warranty. The upgrade is £280 on a Nitron kit (supplied with Nitron springs as standard) or £120 on a Penske kit (supplied with Eibach as standard), excluding VAT. Both standard springs are good; Hyperco is the choice if you want the most consistent and precisely rated spring under the car.</p>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>What is a digressive piston and why does it matter?</summary>
          <p>In a linear piston, damping force rises in a straight line: gentle at low damper speeds, much firmer at high speeds such as potholes and kerbs. A digressive piston blends that force off at the high-speed end, so the car is not harsh over sharp inputs, while retaining strong control at the low speeds where the driver actually feels the car. On the Caterham Seven our preferred configuration is front digressive/linear and rear linear, chosen for how the car loads each axle.</p>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>Are my standard Caterham dampers worn out?</summary>
          <p>They are rarely failed. Standard and OE-style performance dampers are built for broad road use and long life, not for a light, fast Seven being driven hard, so they are typically a little under-damped for the way you now drive and can vary from unit to unit. They are simply doing a different job. If you want to know exactly where yours stand, they can be dyno tested.</p>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>What is the lead time on a Caterham suspension kit?</summary>
